  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Sunday)\nIssued at 1235 PM CDT Mon Sep 14 2026\n\nA warm day is on display across the region with current\ntemperatures in the lower 90s and dewpoints in the low to mid 70s\nas a 594dam is nearly centered over the Mid-South. With surging\ndewpoints and height rises, a Heat Advisory will remain in effect\nacross the entire area through tomorrow evening. Despite height\nrises, peak heating will provide enough lift for a few pop-up\nshowers and thunderstorms with the potential to produce localized\nheavy downpours and gusty winds mainly south of the Tennessee and\nMississippi state line this afternoon. NBM is notorious for not\nhandling summertime convection very well, therefore isolated to\nscattered PoPs were added through the afternoon. Instability will\nwane shortly after sunset resulting in dissipating chances.\n\nBy tomorrow, the aforementioned ridge will broaden to around\n597dam keeping us hot and drying us out. The current Heat\nAdvisory will need to be extended into at least Wednesday,\nhowever, dry air advecting in may result in northeast Mississippi\nfalling just shy of the 105 degree or higher heat index criteria.\nThe extension of this product will be punted to the next forecast\npackage at earliest given uncertainty over northeast Mississippi.\nDeterministic solutions have heights building to 599dam by late\nWednesday and into Thursday with highs spanning the upper 90s to\nlower 100s through at least Saturday. Despite near record\nbreaking highs, Heat Advisories may not be warranted past\nThursday as dry air continues to filter in with persistent\nridging. Beginning Thursday, forecast dewpoints span the 60s to\nlower 70s leading to hot and dry conditions as opposed to hot and\nhumid conditions. This is not great news for ongoing drought\nconcerns as little to no precipitation is expected through the\nend of the week.\n\nLate Saturday, the upper and surface level patterns become less\ndefined as ensemble members are hinting at persistent ridging\nbeginning to flatten out and Gulf moisture beginning to filter\nback in. Isolated to scattered rain chances return across the Mid-\nSouth with increased moisture and a potential cold front pushing\nin. Though a lot can change over the coming days, we look to\nfinally cool closer to normal with highs in the lower 90s and a\nwetter period on the way. One can hope heading into the latter\nhalf of September here in the Mid-South.\n\nAEH",
